{"episodes":{"196892":"An approaching area of surface low pressure with an associated warm and cold front allowed for some areas of instability across southern portions of the Laurel Highlands and south-central Pennsylvania during the late afternoon and evening hours of November 20, 2024. This allowed for gusty showers and an occasional thunderstorm that produced isolated instances of tree and wire damage across Cambria County. Winds ahead of and behind the cold front allowed for trees and wires to fall in portions of Somerset County, where one tree fell on a roadway and a driver drove into a tree, resulting in a fatality.","196910":"The first atmospheric river of the winter storm season created flooding across the North Bay between November 20 and 23. The atmospheric river was accompanied by a very rapidly intensifying cyclone that deepened by 66 millibars within 24 hours, easily meeting the criteria for bomb cyclogenesis. The greatest impacts were concentrated across the North Bay as the river stalled over the region. The North Bay valleys received nearly a foot of rain in three days and the mountains received as much as 22.57 inches (from the Venado gauge).","197286":"A line of strong thunderstorms moved across the coastal waters ahead of a cold front during the evening of November 19th. Several wind gusts of 34 knots or higher were measured along the coast.","197544":"Vigorous post-frontal convection then supported a couple waterspouts along the coast the afternoon of November 20th.","197549":"Heavy rain resulted in damage to Highway 6 around 15 minutes west of Banks in Washington County.","197930":"Deep upper level low pressure moved into the western Great Lakes on November 20th. A strong shortwave rotating around this upper low across Ohio helped to drive a cold front across the Upper Ohio Valley during the late afternoon hours. A thin line of showers accompanied the front, with little to no lightning. The front worked with sufficient instability aloft to bring strong wind gusts to the surface, with several in the 45 to 60 MPH range according to various observations. These gusts were responsible for the many reports of wind damage received across southwest Pennsylvania. Strong low-level shear and weakly veered wind profiles also supported a brief tornado on the north side of Pittsburgh.","198200":"The passage of a cold front along with associated tropospheric folding will lower snow levels. Instability on the back side of this front will also bring severe weather conditions."},"events":[{"begin":"2024-11-20T00:00Z","county":"Sonoma","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.01K","deaths":0,"end":"2024-11-23T00:00Z","endLat":38.46,"endLon":-122.71,"episode":196910,"id":1220535,"injuries":0,"lat":38.46,"location":"0.88 NW Santa Rosa","lon":-122.71,"narrative":"A total of 12.47 of rain was observed at the Santa Rosa COOP station from November 19 at 4 PM to November 22 at 4 PM. This marks the wettest three-day period on record for the station.","source":"COOP Observer","state":"CA","type":"Heavy Rain","wfo":"MTR"},{"begin":"2024-11-20T00:05Z","county":"Coastal Waters From Oklaloosa-Walton County Line To Mexico Beach Out 20 Nm","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2024-11-20T00:05Z","endLat":30.1854,"endLon":-85.7289,"episode":197286,"id":1220466,"injuries":0,"lat":30.1854,"location":"6.85 ESE Panama City Beach","lon":-85.7289,"magnitude":47.0,"magnitudeType":"MG","source":"Mesonet","state":"Gulf Of Mexico","type":"Marine Thunderstorm Wind","wfo":"TAE"},{"begin":"2024-11-20T00:06Z","county":"Coastal Waters From Oklaloosa-Walton County Line To Mexico Beach Out 20 Nm","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2024-11-20T00:06Z","endLat":30.15,"endLon":-85.67,"episode":197286,"id":1220467,"injuries":0,"lat":30.15,"location":"10.97 ESE Panama City Beach","lon":-85.67,"magnitude":34.0,"magnitudeType":"MG","source":"Mesonet","state":"Gulf Of Mexico","type":"Marine Thunderstorm Wind